\title{Where an object's value comes from}
\description{
A module lists the objects it needs in \code{expectsInput}. This page describes
where the value for each of those objects actually comes from, and how a
module should ask.
}
\details{
There are four possible sources. Three are always available; the fourth has
to be turned on.

\enumerate{
\item \strong{The user}, in the \code{simInit()} call, using \verb{objects =} or \verb{inputs =}.
\item \strong{The module developer}, in the module's own \code{.inputObjects} function.
This is the fallback: it should only supply a value when nothing else will.
\item \strong{Another module}, which lists the same object name in its
\code{createsOutput} and runs earlier.
\item \strong{Another module's \code{init} event, run early}, if
\code{options(spades.allowInitDuringSimInit = TRUE)}. Unlike the third source,
the object is a real value that a \code{.inputObjects} function can use while
working out its own default. See \code{\link[=spadesOptions]{spadesOptions()}}.
}
}
\section{Asking where a value will come from}{


Use \code{\link[=suppliedElsewhere]{suppliedElsewhere()}}. It answers the question "is anybody else going to
provide this?", so a module can skip work that would be wasted:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{.inputObjects <- function(sim) \{
  if (!suppliedElsewhere("flammability", sim)) \{
    sim$flammability <- prepInputs(url = extractURL("flammability"), ...)
  \}
  return(invisible(sim))
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

With \code{returnWhere = TRUE} it reports each source separately, as
\code{userSupplied}, \code{prevDotInputObjects} and \code{inFutureInit}.
}

\section{Why \code{is.null()} is not enough}{


It is tempting to write \verb{if (is.null(sim$flammability))} instead. That is
wrong whenever another module is going to create the object, because that
module has not run yet when \code{.inputObjects} happens. \code{sim$flammability} really
is \code{NULL} at that moment, so the test passes and the module downloads or
computes a value that is overwritten a moment later. Nothing fails and no
warning appears; the only symptom is wasted work, which for a \code{sourceURL} is a
wasted download on every run. \code{\link[=suppliedElsewhere]{suppliedElsewhere()}} is what tells "nobody has
this" apart from "it is on its way".
}

\section{Deferring to the user in an init event}{


Nothing in \code{SpaDES} decides between a user-supplied object and a
module-produced one. A module gives way to the user only because its
\code{.inputObjects} was written to. An \code{init} event normally assigns the objects
it lists in \code{createsOutput} without checking, since creating them is its job,
so it gives way to nobody. If you want a module's output to step aside for a
value the user supplied, say so, using the same test one level up:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{init = \{
  if (!suppliedElsewhere("studyArea", sim)) \{
    sim$studyArea <- ...   # only work it out if nobody supplied one
  \}
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Each object is resolved on its own}{


None of this is decided per module. Every object name is resolved
independently, so in one run a module can take \code{ageMap} from the user,
\code{biomassMap} from its own default, and \code{landCover} from another module. There
is no such thing as a module being "in default mode".

This is what lets a project grow gradually. Begin with one module and no
arguments: every object falls back to the developer's defaults and it runs on
its own. Supply one object yourself, and only that one changes source. Add a
module that creates another one, and that one changes too. The other modules
are untouched, and no module's code has to change, because the same test that
used to supply a default now simply declines to.

It is also why one module can be both a standalone tool and part of a large
collection of modules. In the first case every object falls back to a default;
in the second most come from other modules. The module cannot tell the
difference, and does not need to.
}

